Vertex Shader 
	Normalized device coordinates between -1,1
	3 dimensional vertices ->


glViewPort is used to transform the NDC coordinates to screen coordinates
	0,0 to WIDTH,HEIGHT


A vertex buffer object allows us to write memory to the GPU using a buffer of vertices
	glBufferData point to the data and describes it

The vertex shader uses GLSL, which has data types of vec3, vec4, etc
	
glVertexAttribPointer
	Describes our data in the VBO to the vertex shader
	Size, type, and size of each array element (this can be set to 0 for automatic detection when data has no padding), 

Vertex Array Objects store calls to glVertexAttribPointer, glEnableVertexAttribArray, and VBOs
	VAOs are usually bound and called per object we wish to render?

glDrawArrays will draw after calling glBindVertexArray(VAO);


Element Buffer Arrays store the order to index an array of vertices to get the desired triangles from them, instead of storing redundant triangles in the array of vertices


Ideas IF hitbox detection ever becomes a performance bottleneck:
	- Object 1 is not moving, only check it's collision against objects that are moving
	- Each object will only check collision with objects that are near to it and will ignore distances over its threshold
	- ???

Hitbox translation, scale, and rotation are done on CPU to replicate transformations of attached objects in the vertex shader


ARCHITECTURAL DECISIONS
 - Meshes are re-used, so structs that contain a mesh pointer do NOT free the mesh pointer when they are destroyed
 - This is relevant to game_objects and hitboxes
 - Assume all meshes are normalized between -1 and 1 so scaling is uniform???
 - HITBOX is square -> Split into quadrants -> apply normal force as rotation_offset_hitbox + rotation_object + integer multiple of PI/2 -> ??? -> Profit?
 - Velocity with x and y comp can be treated as a directional vector, we can transform the direction of the normal force into a vector, add the two, normalize, and then we have the angle of trajectory after a collision


Use function pointers so that the program can determine what should happen on input dynamically?
 - Example: if (gl_key_up) == GLFW_PRESS then process_func_list(struct function_list *functions) else continue;
 - struct function_list{ (void *)fp(struct game_object *); struct function_list *next; struct game_object *subject; };
 - must contain the subject of the determined action with the function that is to be performed?

 Could get REALLY complicated like this... maybe have game_objects store predetermined actions? Like destroy(), move_up, etc?
 Or load instructions from a file at runtime? - less dynamic



